What the Next Phase of Your Agent Architecture Looks Like
Now that your read-and-execute loop is verified, expanding this into an agent capable of inspecting and expanding its own codebase involves a few natural progression steps:
 * Controlled File Writing & Patching: Right now, your tool is strictly read-only regarding code execution (cat, grep, sed). To let it write new modules, you'll want to introduce a companion write tool (e.g., a safe write_file or strict patch-applicator) that enforces directory boundaries and appends file names to your tracking index.
 * Automated Self-Testing Harness: When an agent modifies its own code, it needs a verification gate. You can leverage your existing selftest() function as an automated test harness—if the agent writes a new module or alters sandbox_tool.py, the system can automatically trigger --selftest to ensure no security policies or core behaviors were broken.
 * Modular Expansion: Instead of cramming everything into a single script, the agent can begin spinning off specialized helper modules in the tools directory (like dedicated loggers, database connectors, or market telemetry parsers) while keeping the core runner protected.
